  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nThrough the rest of this afternoon and into the evening, a\ndiffuse zone of cyclonic shear vorticity (currently across \ncentral Illinois) will shift northward across the area. Some \nhigh-based convective cloud structures are already evident with \nthis feature within zone of locally steeper mid-level lapse \nrates and resultant non-zero MUCAPE for parcels rooted above \nabout 800 mb. Hard to say if forcing will be sufficient to \nresult in a localized blossoming additional shower activity \ngiven limited hires support for this, but given the modestly \nincreased instability, can't rule out some isolated brief \ndownpours if sufficient forcing is realized. The lightning \nthreat remains relatively low given progged ELs warmer than \nabout -10 C. \n\nThrough the rest of the evening and overnight, while general \nisentropic upglide is generally forecast to weaken, a moist\nairmass with several mid-level perturbations drifting around \nupstairs will result in at least a low chance for \nsprinkles/showers persisting through the night. Through Sunday \nmorning, another shortwave--this one currently pushing across \neastern Oklahoma--will arrive in our south. This additional wave\nof more coherent forcing should result in a modest expansion of\nlight precipitation once again, particularly for areas south of\nabout I-80 into the afternoon. Soundings farther to the north \nstill look supportive of some very light precip, so have added a\nmention of sprinkles across northern Illinois during the \nafternoon as a result. \n\nMuch farther to the west, a long-wave trough is progged to \neventually consolidate from several disturbances off the west\ncoast, including the current closed low off of the Pacific \nNorthwest. Guidance is in reasonable agreement in bringing the \nlong-wave trough across the Rockies Sunday night with a \ndeepening surface low near 1000 mb developing across the Plains \nof KS/NE. This is expected to induce increasing, broad southerly\nlow-level flow across the eastern Plains and Midwest Monday. \nBest moisture return and precipitation chances look to initially\nbe largely along/west of the Mississippi River, though a period\nof warm advection induced showers appears possible into parts \nof northern IL/WI during the day. Current NBM pops still appear \ntoo high across the area Monday into Monday night, although the \nareal extent has trended downwards a bit. \n\nConfidence then decreases moving into mid-week with the\nevolution of the upper trough, and the track/trends of the \nprimary surface low. Global ensembles continue to show \nsignificant spread in the details of the evolution of the \nprimary circulation within the upper trough (the former Pacific \nNW closed low) and the degree of phasing with a northern-stream \ndisturbance dropping southeast across the Canadian Prairies. \nThis has significant impacts on the track, intensity, location \nand potential occlusion of the primary surface low from Monday \nnight onward. At this time, it does appear that the early/mid- \nweek period will be somewhat unsettled with chances of more \nwidespread rain. Potentially windy and continued warmer than \naverage conditions are also likely especially Monday into \nTuesday. Of lowest confidence is perhaps how quickly the system \ndeparts mid-late week, and the magnitude of any intrusion of \ncooler air as it does.\n\nCarlaw/Ratzer",
